me{sin}⁡(4t)+4\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ +(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\ (-15\\alpha +8\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+(-8\\alpha -15\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) & =-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-15\\alpha +8\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)) \\\\ -8\\alpha -15\\beta &=-6\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{48}{289}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{90}{289}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}+\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+Bt{e}^{-t}$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{48}{289}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4t)+\\frac{90}{289}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{48}{289}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{90}{289}\\right)}^{2}}=\\frac{6}{17}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186372974 :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $2\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=2\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=2I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{7}}=7q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7I=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+7{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +4\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{5}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function ${I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}}=2.\n$$" "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $2\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{7}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $1\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=2\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=2I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{7}}=7q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=1\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)=2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(2I+7q+\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+7I=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+2\\,\\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+7{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+2(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +7(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\ (3\\alpha +4\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-4\\alpha +3\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha +4\\beta &=10\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -4\\alpha +3\\beta &=0\\, && (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{6}{5}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{8}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))+\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The quantity ${I}_{c}(t)={e}^{-t}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{6}\\,t))$ is the *transient current*, since it goes to zero as $t\\to \\infty .$\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle {I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\n$$ is the *steady-state current*. It governs the current's long-term behavior.\n\nTherefore, as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\nI(t)≈{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{6}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{8}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nFinally, the amplitude of the steady-state current, in amperes, is given by\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{8}{5}\\right)}^{2}}=2.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373003 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n|  | $y(x)=$ | $x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}+{y}^{2}\n$$ that satisfies $y(2)=-1.$\n\n|  | $y(x)=$ | $x$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |"] [:db/cas 17592186373125 :question/worked-solution "If we set $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "If we set $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9},\n$$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(0,4)$ can be found by computing $∇f(0,4).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(0,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}x\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}y\\,j \\\\ ∇f(0,4) & =-\\frac{2}{9}(0)\\,i+\\frac{1}{8}(4)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,j \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(0,4)=⟨0,\\frac{1}{2}⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(0,4).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-0)\\frac{1}{2}-(y-4)0 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =0 \\\\ x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373202 :question/problem "What is the center of the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4}=1?$" "What is the center of the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{4}=1?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373209 :question/problem "For the ellipse \n$\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1$, find the lengths of its major and minor axes." "For the ellipse \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}=1,\n$$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes."] [:db/cas 17592186373346 :question/problem "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}=1.$" "Calculate the $x$-intercepts of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373353 :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.$" "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373407 :question/problem "Calculate the point where the ellipse $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}=1$ intercepts the $y$-axis." "Calculate the point where the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{16}=1\n$$ intercepts the $y$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186373596 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}2(5)^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}2(5)^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373596 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=2,$ $r=5$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}2(5)^{n-1} & =2\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{1-5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-15\\,624) \\\\  & =7\\,812\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=2,$ $r=5$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}2(5)^{n-1} & =2\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{1-5} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(-15\\,624) \\\\  & =7\\,812\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373603 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}5(3)^{n}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}5(3)^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373603 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=3$ and $N=6.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5(3)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5(3)^{1} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=15,r=3$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}5(3)^{n} & =15\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{1-3} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}(-728) \\\\  & =5\\,460\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=3$ and $N=6.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=5(3)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =5(3)^{1} \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=15,r=3$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}5(3)^{n} & =15\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{1-3} \\\\  & =15\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}(-728) \\\\  & =5\\,460\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373610 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373610 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=8$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=6,r=8$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n} & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{8}^{5})}{1-8} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{8}^{5})}{(-7)} \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{-32\\,767}{-7}\\right) \\\\  & =28\\,086\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=8$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{1} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=6,r=8$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{3}{4}(8)^{n} & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{8}^{5})}{1-8} \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{(1-{8}^{5})}{(-7)} \\\\  & =6\\left(\\frac{-32\\,767}{-7}\\right) \\\\  & =28\\,086\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186373617 :question/problem "Determine the value of the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." "Determine the value of the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}\n$$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186373617 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5},r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\,124}{3\\,125} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{3\\,125} \\\\  & ≈0.250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places." "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}\\frac{1}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5},r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3\\,124}{3\\,125} \\\\  & =\\frac{781}{3\\,125} \\\\  & ≈0.250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the final answer has been rounded to three decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186374531 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{9\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{9\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374551 :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374551 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=18,r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{32}{243})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{211}{243}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{422}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore, we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=18,r=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}27{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{n} & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{5})}{(1-\\frac{2}{3})} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{32}{243})}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =18\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{211}{243}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{422}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374573 :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{n-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374573 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{1}{3},$ $r=5$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{3}(5)^{n-1} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{6})}{1-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(-15\\,624)}{(-4)} \\\\  & =1\\,302\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}