um }_{n=1}^{5}27{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{243}]}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{242}{243}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =27\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{242}{243} \\\\  & =\\frac{121}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374579 :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following series:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374579 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{7},$ $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{729}]}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{728}{729}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{14}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=\\frac{6}{7},$ $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{6}{7}{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1} & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{[1-\\frac{1}{729}]}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{\\left(\\frac{728}{729}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{7}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{14}\\cdot \\frac{728}{729} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374582 :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374582 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}=4.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=4$,  $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =4\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{625})}{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{624}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{624}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{20}{{5}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{1}=4.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=4$,  $r=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}20\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =4\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{625})}{\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{5}{4}\\cdot \\frac{624}{625} \\\\  & =\\frac{624}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374585 :question/problem "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}=$\n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the **exact value** of the following geometric series:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374585 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2},r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{256})}{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{255}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{425}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{10}{{4}^{n}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore the first term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{5}{2},r=\\frac{1}{4}$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}10\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{4}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{256})}{\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{255}{256} \\\\  & =\\frac{425}{128}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374588 :question/problem "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{6}7{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{6}7{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{n-1}≈\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374591 :question/problem "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}5{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}≈$\n{{field-1}}" "Determine the value of the following series rounded to $3$ **decimal places.**\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}5{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}≈\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186374607 :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$" "$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\frac{2{log}_{4}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374617 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡x}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186374624 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{e}^{{e}^{2}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{2x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375061 :question/worked-solution "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}.$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$" "We start by integrating the acceleration to get the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $C,$ we use the fact that $v(0)=1.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+C \\\\ 1 & =-\\frac{1}{2}+C \\\\ C & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the velocity is given by $$\n\\displaystyle v(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate the velocity to get $x(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int (-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t+K\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo determine $K$, we use the fact that $x(0)=0.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+\\frac{3}{2}(0)+K \\\\ K & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the position $x$ of the particle at time $t$ is \n\n$$\nx(t)=-\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\frac{3}{2}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375064 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{3\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186375067 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}{5x}\\,\\text{d}x=\n$$ \n{{field-1}}"] [:db/cas 17592186375070 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=($\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$" "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{2}^{8}\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=(\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$"] [:db/cas 17592186375167 :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}$ that satisfies $y(0)=e.$" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{xy}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡y}\n$$ that satisfies $y(0)=e.$"] [:db/cas 17592186375201 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}9(3)^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}9(3)^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375201 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=9,$ $r=3$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}9(3)^{n-1} & =9\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{1-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(-728) \\\\  & =3\\,276\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=9,$ $r=3$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}9(3)^{n-1} & =9\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{6})}{1-3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}(-728) \\\\  & =3\\,276\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375214 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375214 :question/worked-solution "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{63}{64}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Notice that we can rewrite the series as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nSince the above series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, ${a}_{1}=1,$ $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $N=6.$ If we substitute these values into the above formula, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{{2}^{n-1}} & =1\\cdot \\frac{[1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{6}]}{(1-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{63}{64}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{63}{32}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375221 :question/problem "Determine the value of the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}$ rounded to $3$ decimal places." "Determine the value of the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{5}8{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n-1}\n$$ rounded to $3$ decimal places."] [:db/cas 17592186375247 :question/problem "The geometric series $\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{54}+\\frac{1}{162}$ can be expressed using sigma notation as" "The geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{1}{54}+\\frac{1}{162}\n$$ can be expressed using sigma notation as"] [:db/cas 17592186375300 :question/worked-solution "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & =4\\,⟹\\,x=4-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-z)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (16-8z+{z}^{2})+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+16 & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+16 & =18 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+8 & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2({z}^{2}-4z+4) & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2(z-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{5}=1.$" "Since we want to find the projection onto the $yz$-plane, we need to eliminate the variable $x.$ First, we solve for $x$ in the equation of the plane, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+z & =4\\,⟹\\,x=4-z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the expression for $x$ into the equation of the sphere, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4-z)^{2}+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (16-8z+{z}^{2})+(y-1)^{2}+{z}^{2} & =18 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+16 & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by completing the square for the variable $z$, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+16 & =18 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2{z}^{2}-8z+8 & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2({z}^{2}-4z+4) & =10 \\\\ (y-1)^{2}+2(z-2)^{2} & =10 \\\\ \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{5} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the projection of the intersection is the ellipse $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{10}+\\frac{(z-2)^{2}}{5}=1.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375342 :question/problem "What is the value of ${\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{log}_{8}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?$" "What is the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{6{log}_{8}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x?\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375349 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}4(5)^{n}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}4(5)^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375349 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=5$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4(5)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(5)^{1}=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=20,r=5$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}4(5)^{n} & =20\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{1-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{4}(-624) \\\\  & =-5(-624) \\\\  & =3\\,120\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=5$ and $N=4.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=4(5)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =4(5)^{1}=20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=20,r=5$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}4(5)^{n} & =20\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{1-5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{20}{4}(-624) \\\\  & =-5(-624) \\\\  & =3\\,120\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375356 :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n}.$" "Evaluate the series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375356 :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=4$ and $N=6.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{1}=\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5},r=4$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{4}^{6})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\frac{-4\\,095}{-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(1\\,365) \\\\  & =1\\,092.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "Since the given series is in the form $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}a{r}^{n},\n$$ it is a geometric series. \n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=4$ and $N=6.$ We need to figure out the value of ${a}_{1}.$\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n},\n$$\n\nand therefore we can calculate the first term ${a}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{1}=\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{1}=\\frac{4}{5},r=4$ and $N=6$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{5}(4)^{n} & =\\frac{4}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{4}^{6})}{1-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}\\left(\\frac{-4\\,095}{-3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5}(1\\,365) \\\\  & =1\\,092.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375363 :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}4{(5)}^{n-1}.$" "Evaluate the geometric series $$\n\\displaystyle {\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}4{(5)}^{n-1}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375418 :question/problem "Given the **orthonormal** basis $B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}$  of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$" "Given the **orthonormal** basis $$\n\\displaystyle B={\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix},\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{bmatrix}}\n$$  of ${R}^{2},$ find $[x]_{B}$ if $x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.$"] [:db/cas 17592186375432 :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" "Given that ${v}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{bmatrix}$ and ${v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$"] [:db/cas 17592186375659 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+2(5) \\\\  & =12, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-7{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-7(5)-(12) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.$" "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,\\underbrace{(LU)}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL\\underbrace{(Ux)}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly &=b \\\\ Ux &=y.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 7 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} &=5 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} &=2 \\\\ 7{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}+{y}_{3} &=-1.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =5, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =2+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =2+2(5) \\\\  & =12, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =-1-7{y}_{1}-{y}_{2} \\\\  & =-1-7(5)-(12) \\\\  & =-48.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$$\n\\displaystyle y=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 12 \\\\ -48\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375694 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix}$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -9 & -16\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ \nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{12}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186375701 :question/problem "Given that\n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$" "Given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 2 \\\\ 12 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$"] [:db/cas 17592186375783 :question/problem "![](images/ee/ee7e86838a15a0a5c223beaca7e02628123937afb921408d602ad135fbdc0584.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/ee/ee7e86838a15a0a5c223beaca7e02628123937afb921408d602ad135fbdc0584.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375790 :question/problem "![](images/6f/6f1cd478f596aa904dc9b1e254c2eb2873b264935cc11b2c3480fc3560d08d55.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/6f/6f1cd478f596aa904dc9b1e254c2eb2873b264935cc11b2c3480fc3560d08d55.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-4}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375797 :question/problem "![](images/52/5203ef7c7c2bd96ff42d3388682fcb1b2c8fd57697c6d1185937eeef74fde209.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/52/5203ef7c7c2bd96ff42d3388682fcb1b2c8fd57697c6d1185937eeef74fde209.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375979 :question/problem "![](images/33/331abc8bb1e445dd27a8d888872d3292786f0d9bf8d9e0e258cdd15f041afba4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $1.$ Find the value of \n${\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/33/331abc8bb1e445dd27a8d888872d3292786f0d9bf8d9e0e258cdd15f041afba4.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of a line segment and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(4,0)$ with radius $1.$ Find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375986 :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Calculate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle \\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186375986 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate  $\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10D \\\\  & =(9-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$" "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=5{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=10x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 5{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(5{x}^{2}+1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)-\\int (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)(10x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate  $$\n\\displaystyle \\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(x)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\int (\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant.\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+D) \\\\  & =-(5{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10D \\\\  & =(9-5{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+10x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=10D.$"] [:db/cas 17592186376000 :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6fda8f25a1b25a0cbb128e2b5f1bf391d3a49d77ce81175ed85e362d8d89d5d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments, an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $1$, and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(3,0)$ with radius $3.$ Find the value of ${\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/e6/e6fda8f25a1b25a0cbb128e2b5f1bf391d3a49d77ce81175ed85e362d8d89d5d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments, an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(0,0)$ with radius $1$, and an arc of a quarter-circle centered at $(3,0)$ with radius $3.$ Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186376021 :question/problem "![](images/1c/1c42152de7cf176fa122ddf48aad7d93fe982ec1bc2717e3048d9ff5ea45f302.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(6).$" "![](images/1c/1c42152de7cf176fa122ddf48aad7d93fe982ec1bc2717e3048d9ff5ea45f302.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{1}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(6).$"] [:db/cas 17592186376028 :question/problem "![](images/ec/ec8492d2d545c54f8af112d2221c09eeb0201ca9cdc199d1c6b2cab768c282ea.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,$ determine the value of $g(1).$" "![](images/ec/ec8492d2d545c54f8af112d2221c09eeb0201ca9cdc199d1c6b2cab768c282ea.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Given $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)={\\int }_{-4}^{t}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x,\n$$ determine the value of $g(1).$"] [:db/cas 17592186376075 :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}]-[{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”." "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}]-[{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1\n$$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1;\n$$ “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."] [:db/cas 17592186376552 :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, ${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.$" "Recall that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nmeans that\n\n$$\n∀ε>0,\\,∃N\\in N,∀n\\in N,\\,n\\ge N\\,⇒\\,|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nLet's write this in words.\n\nWe need to show that for any real $ε>0,$ there exists a natural number $N$ such that, for every natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε.\n$$\n\nBefore we start the proof, we would like to figure out how $N$ may depend on $ε.$\n\nFirst, simplifying $|{a}_{n}-L|,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|{a}_{n}-L| & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}+\\frac{1}{3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3(1-{n}^{2})}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}+\\frac{3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{3-3{n}^{2}+3{n}^{2}-7}{3(3{n}^{2}-7)}| \\\\  & =|-\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}>0$ for any natural $n\\ge 2.$ With that in mind, we have\n\n$$\n|{a}_{n}-L|=|\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}|=\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21}.\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the inequality $|{a}_{n}-L|<ε$ for $n\\ge 2:$\n\nSolving this inequality for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} & <ε \\\\ 4 & <ε(9{n}^{2}-21) \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε} & <9{n}^{2}-21 \\\\ \\frac{4}{ε}+21 & <9{n}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{4}{9ε}+\\frac{7}{3} & <{n}^{2} \\\\ n & >\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, if we want $$\n\\displaystyle |\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)|<ε,\n$$ it's sufficient to take all values of $n$ that are larger than $\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nWe can now continue the proof:\n\nTherefore, we choose $N>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}.$\n\nNotice that, since $\\frac{4}{9ε}>0,$ then $$\n\\displaystyle \\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}}>\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}}≈1.53\n$$ and therefore $N\\ge 2.$\n\nWith that in mind, we'll find the upper bound for the absolute value of the sequence's $n$th term:\n\nThen, for any natural number $n\\ge N,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)| & =\\frac{4}{9{n}^{2}-21} \\\\  & <\\frac{4}{9{(\\sqrt{\\frac{7}{3}+\\frac{4}{9ε}})}^{2}-21} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(21+\\frac{4}{ε}-21)} \\\\  & =ε.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make the conclusion:\n\nTherefore, according to the definition, $$\n\\displaystyle {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{1-{n}^{2}}{3{n}^{2}-7}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377013 :question/problem "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\operatorname{cos}⁡({J}_{3}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(\\pi ))=\\,\\,$ |  | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | {{field-4}} | $0$ |  |\n|  | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ |  |  |\n|  |  | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | {{field-5}} |  |" "Let ${J}_{n}(\\lambda )$ denote an $n\\times n$ Jordan block with $\\lambda $ on the main diagonal. Fill in the missing entries in the matrix below.\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} | $0$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $$\n\\displaystyle \\operatorname{