oceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 12{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}(4{x}^{3})-\\int (4{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{9}-8D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =[{\\left(\\frac{e}{2}\\right)}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & \\,-[{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{8}{9})]-[\\frac{1}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{9})]-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=12{x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 12{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=4{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}(4{x}^{3})-\\int (4{x}^{3})\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)-\\int \\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-8(\\frac{{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{3}+\\frac{8{x}^{3}}{9}-8D \\\\  & ={x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-8D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/2}^{e/2}12{x}^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[{x}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)+\\frac{8}{9})]}_{1/2}^{e/2} \\\\  & =[{\\left(\\frac{e}{2}\\right)}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{e}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & \\,-[{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{3}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}))}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2})+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+\\frac{8}{9})]-[\\frac{1}{8}(4{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+\\frac{8}{9})] \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{3}}{8}(4-\\frac{8}{3}+\\frac{8}{9})]-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{8}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{e}^{3}-2}{18}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377568 :question/problem "![](images/01/017fd41508a91d5674af92b280fd73690357645428279e09e8633fe70ce3278d.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n${\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/01/017fd41508a91d5674af92b280fd73690357645428279e09e8633fe70ce3278d.png)\n\nGiven the graph of $y=f(x)$ above, find the value of \n$$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-3}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377594 :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "Evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377594 :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D) \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={(x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(e{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2e)-(1{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+2(1)) \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\n$$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=1\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\,\\text{d}x=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x))(x)-\\int (x)\\left(\\frac{1}{x}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2(x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)-x+D) \\\\  & =x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-2D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={(x{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+2x)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =(e{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-2e\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+2e)-(1{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+2(1)) \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377601 :question/problem "Calculate $F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Calculate $F\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle F(x)={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377608 :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b9aab0fb479571175b957870ceec3bf0c7bf7de026f45333f4f11bb062bc4c1.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" "![](images/2b/2b9aab0fb479571175b957870ceec3bf0c7bf7de026f45333f4f11bb062bc4c1.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $f(x),$ shown above. It consists of three line segments. Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{-1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377668 :question/problem "Let \n$A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." "Let \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply."] [:db/cas 17592186377715 :question/problem "Calculate $F(3)$ where $F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}(4t-3)\\,\\text{d}t.$" "Calculate $F(3)$ where $$\n\\displaystyle F(x)={\\int }_{1}^{x}(4t-3)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377756 :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.$" "Find $\\text{rank}(A)$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle \\text{Null}(A)=\\{\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\,|\\,\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R\\}.\n$$"] [:db/cas 17592186377893 :question/worked-solution "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.$ only" "The Chinese remainder theorem states that if the natural numbers ${n}_{1},{n}_{2},…,{n}_{k}$ are pairwise coprime, meaning that $\\text{gcd}({n}_{i},{n}_{j})=1$ whenever $i\\ne j,$ then for any integers ${a}_{1},{a}_{2},…,{a}_{k}$ the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡{a}_{1} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{1}) \\\\ x≡{a}_{2} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{2}) \\\\ \\,⋮ \\\\ x≡{a}_{k} & (\\text{mod}\\,{n}_{k})\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas a unique solution modulo ${n}_{1}{n}_{2}⋯{n}_{k}.$\n\nLet's now inspect each of the given systems in turn.\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡4 & (\\text{mod}\\,6) \\\\ x≡5 & (\\text{mod}\\,8)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ since $6$ and $8$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(6,8)=2>1\n$$\n\n- The theorem applies to system $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$. The numbers $3,$ and $16$ are pairwise coprime natural numbers. Therefore, according to the Chinese remainder theorem, the system has a unique solution modulo $3\\cdot 16=48.$\n\n- The theorem does *not* apply to system $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡3 & (\\text{mod}\\,4) \\\\ x≡7 & (\\text{mod}\\,12)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ since $4$ and $12$ are not coprime: \n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(4,12)=4>1\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x≡2 & (\\text{mod}\\,3) \\\\ x≡14 & (\\text{mod}\\,16)\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$ only"] [:db/cas 17592186378271 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\\\\\,2<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(0,3).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(3,2).$\n\n- The third set is *open* since it doesn't contain any point of its boundary (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0\\le x<6,\\\\\\,2<y\\le 4\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186378278 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(6,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(8,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,6\\le x<9,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *neither open nor closed*. It contains some of its boundary points, for example, the point $(6,1).$ On the other hand, it does not contain some other boundary points, for example, the point $(8,0).$\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\n- The third set is also *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (the corresponding inequality is strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,6\\le x<9,\\\\\\,0<y<2\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186378415 :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{5}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3}{8{x}^{5}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{8+0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$" "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{5}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{5},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}+3}{8{x}^{5}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(\\frac{8{x}^{5}}{{x}^{5}}+\\frac{x}{{x}^{5}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}},\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}},\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\n$$ and $\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}+\\frac{3}{{x}^{5}})}{(8+\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}})} \\\\  & \\to \\frac{0+0}{8+0-0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the horizontal asymptote is $y=0.$"] [:db/cas 17592186378705 :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)}=2.$" "Let ${a}_{1},{a}_{2}$ be the columns of $A.$\n\nFirst, we find an orthonormal basis of $\\text{Col}(A)=\\text{Span}{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process.\n\n**Step 1**. We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\n**Step 2**. We find the vector ${v}_{2}$ orthogonal to $\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}$ using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-{\\text{proj}}_{{v}_{1}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we normalize ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{q}_{1} & =\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {q}_{2} & =\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{6}}\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & ={Q}^{T}\\,A \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain the following QR factorization of $A:$\n\n$$\n\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}}^{A}=\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{Q}\\overbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{3} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ 0 & \\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{bmatrix}}^{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{r}_{22}}{{q}_{12}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}\\right)}=2.\n$$"] [:d