\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,1\\le x\\le e,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\\\\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\\\\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{3}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186378864 :question/worked-solution "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<6-2x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$; $\\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1<x<4,\\\\\\,x<y<{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ only." "Recall that:\n\n- A set is *open* if it does not contain any of its boundary points. In other words, an open set consists only of the interior points of the set.\n\n- A set is *closed* if it contains **all** of its boundary points. In other words, a closed set consists of all the interior and all the boundary points of the set.\n\nLet's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- The first set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The second set is *open* since it doesn't contain any of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are strict).\n\n- The third set is *closed* since it contains all of its boundary points (all the corresponding inequalities are not strict).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }0<x<3,\\\\\\,0<y<6-2x\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered};\n$$ $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{gathered}\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\\\\\text{ }1<x<4,\\\\\\,x<y<{x}^{2}\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}\n$$ only."] [:db/cas 17592186378892 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z-1)^{2}}{4}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186378899 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $(x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}=1$, find its intercepts with the $x$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle (x+1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-2)^{2}}{8}+\\frac{(z+2)^{2}}{8}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $x$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186378906 :question/problem "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=-1,$ find its $z$-intercepts." "Given the hyperboloid of two sheets $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2}-\\frac{{z}^{2}}{25}=-1,\n$$ find its $z$-intercepts."] [:db/cas 17592186378913 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $\\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1$, find its intercepts with the $z$-axis." "Given the ellipsoid $$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{(x-1)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{z}^{2}}{18}=1,\n$$ find its intercepts with the $z$-axis."] [:db/cas 17592186378941 :question/problem "Given the ellipsoid $(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{(y-1)^{2}}{4}+\\frac{(z-5)^{2}}{2}=1$, find the domain of $z=z(x,y).$" "Given the ellipsoid $$